      "content": "but you're talking about a \"pragmatic middle ground\" when companies like ours actually have the power to fix this. if we just \"adjust for barriers\" without aiming for actual parity, aren't we just performing 'ethics theater' to satisfy ESG reports while the status quo stays the same? it feels like the most honest way to fulfill an ESG mandate is to explicitly prioritize equality of outcome so we can prove we're actually making an impact. shouldn't the ethical choice be to move beyond just 'adjusting' and actually commit to equitable results?",
